{"chunk_id": "69a2b002c227", "text": "In this section, Duryodhana defiantly refuses Krishna\u2019s plea for peace and denies any wrongdoing toward the Pandavas. He argues that the Kshatriya code demands unwavering pride in battle and declares he would rather die on a bed of arrows than submit. Most famously, he swears he will not grant the Pandavas even land equal to the tip of a needle. Krishna responds with fierce anger, listing Duryodhana\u2019s many crimes: the rigged dice game, the humiliation of Draupadi, attempts to burn the Pandavas alive, and repeated plots to kill them. He condemns Duryodhana\u2019s refusal to restore their rightful share and warns that war and destruction will follow. The confrontation reveals the moral divide\u2014Krishna representing justice and memory of wrongs, while Duryodhana embodies pride, denial, and the stubborn resolve that makes the coming war inevitable.\nDuryodhana refuses to give the Pandavas any land; Krishna rebukes him by recounting his injustices, but Duryodhana angrily storms out.\nThemes: arrogance and pride, justice and wrongdoing, failed diplomacy, kshatriya honor, inevitability of war", "vector": [-0.008957995101809502, -0.018653549253940582, -0.012813419103622437, 0.005089160520583391, -0.018465807661414146, 0.01794281043112278, -0.020678484812378883, 0.006376537028700113, -0.032398976385593414, -0.009776015765964985, 0.028188182041049004, -0.0035536957439035177, 0.01881447248160839, 0.016937047243118286, -0.004354953300207853, 0.006037930026650429, 0.04800841584801674, -0.012397704645991325, 0.04715016484260559, -0.026243707165122032, -0.010285601951181889, 0.0008280781330540776, -0.006601157132536173, 0.01595810428261757, -0.0010996341006830335, 0.03827263042330742, 0.00021917250705882907, -0.026042554527521133, 0.013799067586660385, -0.01495234202593565, -0.006245787721127272, 0.0025429038796573877, 0.016735894605517387, -0.007918707095086575, -0.048491183668375015, 0.004281197674572468, -0.014442755840718746, 